# .gitattributes — repo-wide file attributes.
#
# Judge ledgers: let concurrent appends concatenate instead of conflicting.
#
# Both files are JSONL written by the two judge subagents across many repos, worktrees and
# parallel sessions. Two branches each appending a row to the same ledger is the normal case here,
# not an anomaly, and a plain three-way merge turns every one of those into a textual conflict over
# the last line. Git's built-in `union` driver keeps both sides' lines.
#
# ⚠️ These files are NOT append-only. An earlier revision of this comment said they were, and used
# that to argue the duplicate risk below was merely "not expected". A merged PR falsified it: see
# the second LIMIT.
# No custom merge script: a driver that only ever needs "keep both, in order" is exactly what
# the built-in already does, and a script would be one more thing to install per clone.
#
# Two exact literals, not `coding-memory/*/verdicts.jsonl`. A third judge is hypothetical, and
# the attribute should be no wider than the rule it encodes (same call as the git-guard
# allowlist — see docs/features/judge-ledger-commitability.md, "Open questions").
#
# LIMIT — this does NOT close the failure that prompted it. That one was *uncommitted local
# changes* meeting a FAST-FORWARD pull ("Your local changes to the following files would be
# overwritten by merge"). No merge driver runs on a fast-forward, so `merge=union` is inert
# there; it is a branch-vs-branch mechanism only. The half that stops that drift accumulating is
# the git-guard allowlist change, which lets the ledgers be committed on the default branch in
# the first place. Neither half substitutes for the other.
#
# LIMIT — union concatenates, it does not deduplicate. Measured, that is narrower than "any
# byte-identical row duplicates": when both sides append the SAME single row, the three-way merge
# resolves the identical change itself and one row comes out, no driver involved. A byte-identical
# row survives twice only when it sits in two hunks the merge cannot align.
#
# LIMIT — and the shape that actually bit, which the paragraph above does not cover. When one side
# REWRITES an existing row in place and the other keeps the old copy, union keeps BOTH: the stale
# row and the rewritten row, same identity, different bytes. The merge is clean, there is no
# conflict marker, every row still parses, and a duplicate-LINE check sees nothing, because the two
# copies are not identical. It is visible only by grouping rows on (ts, head_sha, stage).
#
# Not hypothetical. Measured 2026-09-17 merging PR #106 into this branch: #106 backfilled the
# `outcome` field on 5 EXISTING rows rather than appending, and all 5 came out of the merge twice.
# The previous wording of this comment predicted exactly this shape and rated it "not expected" on
# the append-only premise; it happened on the first real merge after that sentence was written.
# So: `union` is right for the concurrent-append case it was chosen for, and it is NOT safe across
# an in-place row edit. Detecting that automatically is deliberately out of scope here and is
# tracked as its own card, rather than bolted onto this one.
#
# Everything above is pinned by gitattributes.test.sh, which merges real commits in a throwaway repo
# rather than asserting any of it from these comments: the union behaviour itself (sections 1-5),
# both duplication shapes (6 for a byte-identical row, 10 for an in-place row edit), and the
# fast-forward limit (7). Stated once, here, deliberately — an earlier revision made this claim in
# two adjacent paragraphs with different wording, which is how the two drift apart.
coding-memory/observability-judge/verdicts.jsonl merge=union
coding-memory/compliance-judge/verdicts.jsonl    merge=union
